ed, and sponsor-skewed polling leaves real uncertainty"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a comfortable Hyde-Smith win: a deep-red state, incumbency, and a Likely-to-Solid R consensus, with the only recent poll showing her up six and markets pricing her near 90%. But Senate Democrats' late ad spending, Harris campaigning for Colom, and headlines about Republicans worrying the state is in play justify nudging the margin a few points closer than the baseline.", "watch": "Any fresh independent poll showing Colom within single digits, or further GOP outside spending, would signal the race is genuinely tightening.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 68 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Cindy Hyde-Smith (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Senator Cindy Hyde-Smith seeks reelection in a state that leaned Republican by eleven points at the presidential level. She has raised about $4.5 million and holds a cash advantage over Democrat Scott Colom, a former district attorney who has proven a strong fundraiser at $3.9 million. An independent candidate, Ty Pinkins, trails far behind in money and visibility.\n\nEvery major race rater places the contest at Likely R or better, and prediction markets give Hyde-Smith roughly a nine-in-ten chance. Still, the race has drawn unusual late attention: national Democratic groups have begun advertising, Kamala Harris has campaigned for Colom, and reports suggest Republicans are wary. Polling is sparse and partly partisan-sponsored, including one fabricated poll claim, so uncertainty is elevated. The expected outcome remains a Republican win by high single digits, with a small but nontrivial chance of an upset.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0354, "margin": -10.93}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0478, "margin": -11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0452, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0712, "margin": -10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0743, "mar